INT. DUCKPIN BOWLING ALLEY - NIGHT

A dim neighborhood lane. A faded banner reads "STATE FINALS." Scoreboards flicker. A cigarette machine hums beside the shoe counter.

COACH RAY SALAZAR, 60s, tracksuit, a whistle around his neck, paces behind lane six. IMANI PETTIGREW, 17, ponytail, chalk on her hands, holds a small black ball. DMITRI AKSENOV, 18, her anchor, sits on the ball return chewing his thumbnail.

RAY
Pettigrew. Last frame.

IMANI
I know, Coach.

DMITRI
She knows.

RAY
I'm talking to her.

DMITRI
She's right here, Coach. You can talk to both of us.

RAY
Imani, you need a spare.

IMANI
Ray, I need to breathe first.

RAY
Breathe fast.

DMITRI
Don't rush her.

RAY
Dmitri, sit down.

DMITRI
I am sitting down.

Imani steps to the line. The pin deck waits. She raises the ball to her chin, closes her eyes, and exhales.

RAY
Eyes open!

IMANI
Coach!

DMITRI
Ray, let her--

The ball leaves her hand. It rolls straight, slow, and hits the head pin. Seven pins fall. Three stand in a crooked row.

Silence. The pinsetter whirs. Ray's whistle drops from his mouth.

DMITRI (CONT'D)
Seven. You left three.

RAY
She left three.

IMANI
(quietly)
I have one more ball.

RAY
You do. And you have me.

She turns. Ray places both hands on her shoulders. Dmitri slides off the ball return and joins them.

DMITRI
Imani, same shot. Corner pin first.

RAY
Don't think. Roll.

IMANI
Both of you stop talking.

They stop. She picks up the ball, steps to the line, and releases. The ball curves, kisses the left pin, which tips the middle pin, which topples the right. Ten down.

The alley erupts. Ray throws his whistle in the air. Dmitri lifts Imani off the floor.
